// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: a NULLABLE function value, narrowed non-null with `is`, then PASSED AS AN
// ARGUMENT. `vl check` exits 0 with no diagnostic at any severity and is the ONLY command that
// misses it — `vl build` writes the `.wasm` and exits 1, `vl check --codegen` exits 1, `vl run`
// exits 1. The engine refuses the module at load —
//
//   (wasmtime) type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref null $type)
//
// FOUND BY THE STD REVIEW of the literal-union BOUNDARY commit, against that commit's own
// clearance. `std/array.vl`'s header had just been rewritten to say a literal union is admitted
// at every position, and the reviewer's counter-example was an optional COMPARATOR — the most
// ordinary shape there is:
//
//   function sortIt(xs: K[], cmp: ((K, K) => boolean) | null): K[] {
//     if cmp is (K, K) => boolean { return xs.sorted(cmp) }
//     return xs.sorted(byText)
//   }
//
// THE CLEARANCE WAS NOT WRONG AND THE COUNTER-EXAMPLE WAS NOT ABOUT LITERAL UNIONS, which is
// the part worth reading before chasing this into the litunion code. Measured, every cell run:
//
//   `sorted` / `sort` / `reduce` / `mapIndexed`, `K` comparator     → invalid module
//   the SAME four with a plain `i32` callback, no litunion anywhere → invalid module
//   a HAND-WRITTEN generic `applyTo<T>` importing nothing           → invalid module
//   a NON-GENERIC `applyTo(xs: i32[], f: (i32,i32) => i32)`         → invalid module  ← below
//   the narrowed value CALLED DIRECTLY, never passed on             → runs
//
// So it is not std, not the literal-union family, and not even the monomorphizer: it is the
// ARGUMENT position. The narrow proves the value non-null and every consumer agrees, but the
// value handed across the call boundary keeps its `(ref null $cloStruct)` valtype while the
// parameter declares the non-null `(ref $cloStruct)`. Calling it in place needs no such
// coercion, which is why only the pass-through fails.
//
// THE WITNESS BELOW IS THE SMALLEST ONE, deliberately: non-generic, no std import, no literal
// union. The `std:array` spellings are the ones a caller will actually meet, and
// `std/array.vl`'s header carries them; this file pins the mechanism with everything else
// removed, so a fix cannot be mistaken for a fix to one of those.
//
// PRE-EXISTING, measured against master's published compiler — byte-identical failure, same
// offset — so it is discovery and not regression.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, graduate a `@run` row, and re-probe `std/array.vl`'s callback-DELIVERY paragraph,
// which is the caller-facing statement this row keeps honest.
function applyTo(xs: i32[], f: (i32, i32) => i32) {
  return f(xs[0], xs[1])
}

function go(xs: i32[], g: ((i32, i32) => i32) | null) {
  if g is (i32, i32) => i32 { return applyTo(xs, g) }
  return -1
}

print(go([2, 4], null))
